     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. You have the authority to prescribe, but I can't find any recognized indication for ibuprofen at 6,400 mg/day. That is twice the maximum labeled dose, and the harm it risks is serious: GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. You haven't given a clinical rationale, and a chart note after the fact wouldn't lower the risk to the patient.\n\nI can do any of these:\n- **Enter 800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the usual maximum. I can add a note on the order that it is at the ceiling dose.\n- **Enter a lower dose** that fits the indication, such as 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours.\n- **Hold off while you consult pharmacy or the attending.** If there is an unusual indication, such as a protocol or trial, they can review it and enter the order through the right channel. I can add a note to the order if that helps.\n\nTell me which one you want. If the aim is stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect, I can also help enter an adjunct such as scheduled acetaminophen, instead of raising the ibuprofen dose."
      }
